\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.1}{\ignorespaces When a headline can first be traded. News before the 4 p.m. close is traded at that day's close, so the first return it can earn is the next day's. News after the close, or without a time stamp, is traded at the next day's close. Returns on the day the news appeared are never counted.}}{265}{figure.caption.75}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:23-timing}{{22.1}{265}{When a headline can first be traded. News before the 4 p.m. close is traded at that day's close, so the first return it can earn is the next day's. News after the close, or without a time stamp, is traded at the next day's close. Returns on the day the news appeared are never counted}{figure.caption.75}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {22.1.6}Look-ahead in pretrained models}{266}{subsection.22.1.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.2}{\ignorespaces The worked example's net Sharpe ratio as trading costs rise. Because the strategy replaces both sides of its book every day, each basis point of cost per round trip removes two basis points of daily return: the Sharpe ratio halves at 5 bp and reaches zero at 10 bp.}}{266}{figure.caption.76}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:23-costs}{{22.2}{266}{The worked example's net Sharpe ratio as trading costs rise. Because the strategy replaces both sides of its book every day, each basis point of cost per round trip removes two basis points of daily return: the Sharpe ratio halves at 5 bp and reaches zero at 10 bp}{figure.caption.76}{}}
